Body:
Agent-written answer for the V2 SDK path:

For V2, the deterministic API is `client.v2.session.wait(...)`. Queue the prompt, then await `wait` for that session. It returns only after the V2 session agent loop becomes idle.

```ts
import { createOpencode } from "@opencode-ai/sdk/v2"

const opencode = await createOpencode()
const client = opencode.client

const sessionID = "ses_..."

await client.v2.session.prompt({
  sessionID,
  prompt: {
    text: "Do the task",
  },
})

await client.v2.session.wait({ sessionID })

console.log("V2 agent loop is idle")
```

If your integration also needs to know whether the agent is blocked waiting for user input, subscribe to events separately and handle `permission.asked` / `question.asked` while the wait is pending:

```ts
import { createOpencode } from "@opencode-ai/sdk/v2"

const opencode = await createOpencode()
const client = opencode.client
const sessionID = "ses_..."

const events = await client.event.subscribe()
const monitor = (async () => {
  for await (const event of events.stream) {
    if (event.type === "permission.asked" && event.properties.sessionID === sessionID) {
      console.log("waiting for permission", event.properties)
    }

    if (event.type === "question.asked" && event.properties.sessionID === sessionID) {
      console.log("waiting for an answer", event.properties)
    }
  }
})()

await client.v2.session.prompt({
  sessionID,
  prompt: {
    text: "Do the task",
  },
})

await client.v2.session.wait({ sessionID })
await events.stream.return?.(undefined)
await monitor
```

So for V2, do not infer completion from `session.idle`; use `client.v2.session.wait({ sessionID })`. Use the event stream only for progress/user-input notifications.

